WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500
One of the most exciting recent developments in deep learning has been the rise of end-to-end deep learning.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.400
So what is end-to-end deep learning?

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:13.700
Briefly, there have been some data processing systems or learning systems that require multiple stages of processing.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:21.000
And what end-to-end deep learning does is it can take all those multiple stages and replace it usually with just a single neural network.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Let's look at some examples.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.500
Take speech recognition as an example.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:36.500
Where you go is to take an input X, such as an audio clip, and map it to an output Y, which is a transcript of that audio clip.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:41.500
So traditionally, speech recognition requires many stages of processing.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:46.000
First, you will extract some features, some hand-designed features of the audio clip.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:53.500
So if you've heard of MFCC, that's an algorithm for extracting a certain set of hand-designed features for audio.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:01:01.500
And then having extracted some low-level features, you might apply a machine learning algorithm to find phonemes in the audio clip.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.500
So phonemes are the basic units of sound.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:10.500
So for example, the word cat is made out of three sounds, the k, a, and tuh, so they extract those.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.500
And then you string together phonemes to form individual words.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:18.000
And then you string those together to form the transcript of the audio clip.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:32.500
So in contrast to this pipeline with a lot of stages, what end-to-end deep learning does is you can train a huge neural network to just input the audio clip and have it directly output the transcript.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:39.000
One interesting sociological effect in AI is that as end-to-end deep learning started to work better,

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.500
there were some researchers that had, for example, spent many years of their career designing individual steps of the pipeline.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:52.000
So there were some researchers in different disciplines, not just in speech recognition, maybe in computer vision and other areas as well.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:00.000
They spent a lot of time, you know, written multiple papers, maybe even built a large part of the career, engineering features or entering other pieces of the pipeline.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:09.500
And when end-to-end deep learning just took a large training set and learned the function mapping from X to Y directly, really bypassing a lot of these intermediate steps,

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:17.000
it was challenging for some disciplines to come around to accepting this alternative way of building AI systems.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:23.500
It's because it really obsoleted, in some cases, many years of research in some of the intermediate components.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:30.500
It turns out that one of the challenges of end-to-end deep learning is that you might need a lot of data before it works well.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:40.500
So, for example, if you're training on 3,000 hours of data to build a speech recognition system, then the traditional pipeline, the full traditional pipeline works really well.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:53.000
It's only when you have a very large data set, you know, want to say 10,000 hours of data and anything going up to maybe 100,000 hours of data that the end-to-end approach then suddenly starts to work really well.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:01.000
So when you have a smaller data set, the more traditional pipeline approach actually works just as well, often works even better.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:06.000
And you need a large data set before the end-to-end approach really shines.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:17.500
And if you have a medium amount of data, then there are also intermediate approaches where maybe you input audio and bypass the features and just learn to output the phonemes of a neural network and then have some of the stages as well.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:24.280
So this would be a step toward end-to-end learning, but not all the way there.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:45.830
So this is a picture of a face recognition turnstile built by a researcher, Yun-Ching Lin at Maidu, where this is a camera and it looks at the person approaching the gate and if it recognizes the person, then the turnstile automatically lets them through.

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:55.830
So rather than needing to swipe an RFID patch to enter this facility, in increasingly many offices in China and hopefully more and more in other countries as well,

00:03:55.830 --> 00:04:03.830
you can just approach the turnstile and if it recognizes your face, it just lets you through without needing you to carry an RFID patch.

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:06.830
So how do you build a system like this?

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:11.830
Well, one thing you could do is just look at the image that the camera is capturing.

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:18.830
So I guess this is my bad drawing, but maybe this is a camera image and you have someone approaching the turnstile.

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:22.830
So this might be the image X that your camera is capturing.

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:30.830
And one thing you could do is try to learn a function mapping directly from the image X to the identity of the person Y.

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:33.830
But it turns out this is not the best approach.

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:39.830
And one of the problems is that the person approaching the turnstile could approach from lots of different directions.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:46.830
So they could be in this green position, they could have a blue position, and sometimes they're closer to the camera so they appear bigger in the image.

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:50.830
And sometimes they're already closer to the camera so their face appears much bigger.

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:58.830
So what is actually done to build these turnstiles is not to just take the raw image and feed it to a neural net to try to figure out the person's identity.

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:08.830
Instead, the best approach to date seems to be a multi-step approach where first you run one piece of software to detect the person's face.

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:12.830
So there's first a detector to figure out where is the person's face.

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:27.810
Having detected the person's face, you then zoom into that part of the image, crop that image so that the person's face is centered.

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:34.050
Then it is this picture that I guess I drew here in red.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:40.050
This is then fed to a neural network to then try to learn or estimate the person's identity.

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:45.050
And what researchers have found is that instead of trying to learn everything on one step,

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:51.050
by breaking this problem down into two simpler steps, first is figure out where is the face,

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:54.050
and second is look at the face and figure out who this actually is.

00:05:54.050 --> 00:06:02.050
The second approach allows the learning algorithm or really two learning algorithms to solve two much simpler tasks and results in overall better performance.

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:08.650
By the way, if you want to know how the second step actually works, I've simplified the discussion.

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:13.650
By the way, if you want to know how step two here actually works, I've actually simplified the description a bit.

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:18.650
The way the second step is actually trained is you train a neural network that takes this input two images.

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:27.650
And what the neural network does is it takes this input two images and it tells you if these two are the same person or not.

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:39.670
So if you then have say 10,000 employees IDs on file, you can then take this image in red and quickly compare it against maybe all 10,000 employee IDs on file

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:47.670
to try to figure out if this picture in red is indeed one of your 10,000 employees that you should allow into this facility or that it should allow into your office building.

00:06:47.670 --> 00:06:52.670
If this is a turnstile that is giving employees access to a workplace.

00:06:52.670 --> 00:06:57.670
So why is it that the two-step approach works better? There are actually two reasons for that.

00:06:57.670 --> 00:07:01.670
One is that each of the two problems you're solving is actually much simpler.

00:07:01.670 --> 00:07:08.670
But second is that you have a lot of data for each of the two subtasks.

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:23.450
In particular, there is a lot of data you can obtain for face detection for task one over here where the task is to look at an image and figure out where is the person's face in the image.

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:24.450
So there is a lot of data.

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:31.450
There is a lot of labeled data x,y where x is a picture and y shows the position of the person's face.

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:34.450
So you could build a neural network to do task one quite well.

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:38.450
And then separately, there's a lot of data for task two as well.

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:44.450
Today, leading companies have, let's say, hundreds of millions of pictures of people's faces.

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:49.450
So given a closely cropped image like this red image or this one down here,

00:07:49.450 --> 00:07:58.450
today, leading face recognition teams have at least hundreds of millions of images that they can use to look at two images and try to figure out the identity or figure out if it's the same person or not.

00:07:58.450 --> 00:08:00.450
So there's also a lot of data for task two.

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:14.920
But in contrast, if you were to try to learn everything at the same time, there is much less data of the form x,y where x is an image like this taken from a turnstile and y is the identity of a person.

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:20.910
So because you don't have enough data to solve this end-to-end learning problem,

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:25.910
but you do have enough data to solve subproblems one and two,

00:08:25.910 --> 00:08:33.910
in practice, breaking this down to two subproblems results in better performance than a pure end-to-end deep learning approach.

00:08:33.910 --> 00:08:43.910
Although if you had enough data for the end-to-end approach, maybe the end-to-end approach would work better, but that's not actually what works best in practice today.

00:08:43.910 --> 00:08:46.910
Let's look at a few more examples.

00:08:46.910 --> 00:08:48.910
Take machine translation.

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:58.910
Traditionally, machine translation systems also had a long, complicated pipeline where you first take, say, English text and then do text analysis,

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:07.910
basically extract a bunch of features of the text and so on, and after many, many steps, you then output, say, a translation of the English text into French.

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:17.910
Because for machine translation, you do have a lot of pairs of English comma French sentences, end-to-end deep learning works quite well for machine translation.

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:27.910
And that's because today, it is possible to get a large dataset of XY pairs where that's the English sentence and that's the corresponding French translation.

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:31.910
So in this example, end-to-end deep learning works well.

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:39.910
One last example, let's say that you want to look at an x-ray picture of a hand of a child and estimate the age of a child.

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:45.910
You know, when I first heard about this problem, I thought this is a very cool crime scene investigation task where you find,

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:50.910
maybe tragically, the skeleton of a child and you want to figure out how old the child was.

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:59.910
It turns out that typical application of this problem, estimating age of a child from hand x-ray is less dramatic than this crime scene investigation I was picturing.

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:06.910
It turns out that pediatricians use this to estimate whether or not a child is growing or developing normally.

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:13.910
But a non-end-to-end approach to this would be you look at an image and then you segment out or recognize the bones.

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:18.910
So, you know, just try to figure out where is that bone segment, where is that bone segment, where is that bone segment and so on.

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:29.910
And then knowing the lengths of the different bones, you can sort of go to a lookup table showing the average bone lengths in a child's hand and then use that to estimate the child's age.

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:31.910
And so this approach actually works pretty well.

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:40.910
In contrast, if you were to go straight from the image to the child's age, then you would need a lot of data to do that directly.

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:49.910
And as far as I know, this approach does not work as well today just because there isn't enough data to train this task in an end-to-end fashion.

00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:55.910
Whereas in contrast, you can imagine that by breaking down this problem into two steps.

00:10:55.910 --> 00:10:58.910
Step one is a relatively simple problem.

00:10:58.910 --> 00:10:59.910
Maybe you don't need that much data.

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:02.910
Maybe you don't need that many x-ray images to segment out the bones.

00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:09.910
And task two, well, you know, by collecting statistics of a number of children's hands, you could also get decent estimates of that without too much data.

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:13.910
So this multi-step approach seems, you know, promising.

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:19.910
Maybe more promising than the end-to-end approach, at least until you can get more data for the end-to-end learning approach.

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:29.910
So when end-to-end deep learning works, it can work really well and it can really simplify the system and not require you to build so many hand-designed individual components.

00:11:30.910 --> 00:11:31.910
But it's also not panacea.

00:11:31.910 --> 00:11:33.910
It doesn't always work.

00:11:33.910 --> 00:11:45.910
In the next video, I want to share with you a more systematic description of when you should and maybe when you shouldn't use end-to-end deep learning and how to piece together these complex machine learning systems.
